  from jsonb_array_elements('[{"n":30,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A ball is thrown upwards. Its height in metres after t seconds is h = 20t − 5t².\n\n(a) Calculate the height after 1 second.\n(b) Show that the ball returns to the ground at t = 4.\n(c) State the time at which the ball reaches its maximum height.\n(d) Calculate the maximum height.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A ball is thrown upwards. Its height in metres after t seconds is h = 20t − 5t²."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate the height after 1 second.\n(b) Show that the ball returns to the ground at t = 4.\n(c) State the time at which the ball reaches its maximum height.\n(d) Calculate the maximum height."}]}]},"e":"h(1) = 15 m; the roots are t = 0 and t = 4; the vertex is at t = 2 giving h = 20 m. Beyond t = 4 the formula produces negative heights, so the physical model stops at the landing time.","m":9,"df":"hard","tp":"quadratic functions","st":"quadratic functions","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":30,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"For a quadratic model, roots give the start and end, and the vertex lies halfway between them.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Calculate the height after 1 second, in metres.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Put t = 1 into the height equation, taking care with the squared term.","20(1) − 5(1)²."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":15,"tolerance":0,"unit":"m"},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"State the non-zero time at which the ball returns to the ground.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Set h = 0 and factorise.","5t(4 − t) = 0."],"strand":"D(ii)","answer":4,"tolerance":0,"unit":"s"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"State the time of maximum height, in seconds.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["The maximum is halfway between the two roots.","Half of 4."],"strand":"D(iii)","answer":2,"tolerance":0,"unit":"s"},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Calculate the maximum height, in metres.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Substitute t = 2 into the height equation.","40 − 20."],"strand":"D(iv)","answer":20,"tolerance":0,"unit":"m"},{"id":"e","label":"(e)","prompt":"Explain why the model cannot be used for t = 5.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["What does the equation give at t = 5?","Can a ball be below the ground?"],"strand":"D(iv)","accepted":["at t = 5 the equation gives −25 m, a negative height, and the ball has already landed, so the model only applies until t = 4"],"keywords":[["equation"],["gives"],["negative"],["height"],["ball"],["already"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for factorising, 1 for t = 4. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the midpoint, 1 for 2 s. (d) 2 marks: 1 for substituting, 1 for 20 m. (e) 2 marks: 1 for the negative height, 1 for the valid range.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for factorising, 1 for t = 4. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the midpoint, 1 for 2 s. (d) 2 marks: 1 for substituting, 1 for 20 m. (e) 2 marks: 1 for the negative height, 1 for the valid range.","response_tools":["graph","rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"graph","response_guidance":"Plot h = 20t − 5t² in the workspace and read the vertex from the graph to check (c) and (d)."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for factorising, 1 for t = 4. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the midpoint, 1 for 2 s. (d) 2 marks: 1 for substituting, 1 for 20 m. (e) 2 marks: 1 for the negative height, 1 for the valid range."},{"n":31,"t":"assess_parts","p":"The loudness of a sound in decibels is L = 10 log₁₀(I / I₀), where I₀ is the threshold of hearing.\n\n(a) State the loudness when I = 1 000 I₀.\n(b) State the loudness when I = 10 000 I₀.\n(c) Explain why multiplying the intensity by 10 adds exactly 10 decibels.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The loudness of a sound in decibels is L = 10 log₁₀(I / I₀), where I₀ is the threshold of hearing."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) State the loudness when I = 1 000 I₀.\n(b) State the loudness when I = 10 000 I₀.\n(c) Explain why multiplying the intensity by 10 adds exactly 10 decibels."}]}]},"e":"1000 I₀ gives 30 dB and 10 000 I₀ gives 40 dB. Since log(10x) = log x + 1, each tenfold increase in intensity adds 10 dB, which is why decibels are a logarithmic scale.","m":5,"df":"hard","tp":"logarithms","st":"logarithms","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":31,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","strand":"C(i)","next_time_hint":"A logarithmic scale turns multiplication into addition: a constant factor becomes a constant difference.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"State the loudness when I = 1 000 I₀, in decibels.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["The ratio I/I₀ is 1000.","10 × log(1000) and log(1000) = 3."],"strand":"C(i)","answer":30,"tolerance":0,"unit":"dB"},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"State the loudness when I = 10 000 I₀, in decibels.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["The intensity ratio is now 10 000.","log(10 000) = 4, so evaluate 10 × 4."],"strand":"C(i)","answer":40,"tolerance":0,"unit":"dB"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Explain why a tenfold increase in intensity adds 10 dB.","kind":"text","points":3,"hints":["Use log(10x) = log x + 1.","The formula multiplies the logarithm by 10."],"strand":"C(ii)","accepted":["multiplying the intensity by ten adds 1 to the logarithm, and the formula multiplies the logarithm by 10, so the loudness rises by exactly 10 dB"],"keywords":[["multiplying"],["intensity"],["adds"],["logarithm"],["formula"],["multiplies"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 1 mark. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the log law, 1 for the ×10 in the formula, 1 for the 10 dB conclusion.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 1 mark. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the log law, 1 for the ×10 in the formula, 1 for the 10 dB conclusion.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Quote the logarithm law you use in (c); the mark is for the law, not the arithmetic."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 1 mark. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the log law, 1 for the ×10 in the formula, 1 for the 10 dB conclusion."},{"n":32,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A car loses 15% of its value each year. It cost $24 000 when new.\n\n(a) State the multiplier for one year of depreciation.\n(b) Calculate the value after 3 years, to the nearest dollar.\n(c) Calculate the number of complete years after which the value first falls below $8 000, testing successive years.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A car loses 15% of its value each year. It cost "},{"type":"text","text":"$24 000 when new."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) State the multiplier for one year of depreciation.\n(b) Calculate the value after 3 years, to the nearest dollar.\n(c) Calculate the number of complete years after which the value first falls below "},{"type":"text","text":"$8 000, testing successive years."}]}]},"e":"Multiplier 0.85. Value after 3 years $14 739 and after 6 years $9 052. After 7 years 9 052 × 0.85 ≈ $7 694, the first value below $8 000.","m":9,"df":"hard","tp":"geometric sequences","st":"geometric sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":32,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"For ''when does the value fall below X'', compute successive values and bracket the threshold.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"State the depreciation multiplier for one year.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["A 15% loss leaves 85% of the value.","Write 85% as a decimal."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":0.85,"tolerance":0.001},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the value after 3 years, to the nearest dollar.","kind":"numeric","points":3,"hints":["Use 24000 × 0.85^3.","0.85³ = 0.614125."],"strand":"D(ii)","answer":14739,"tolerance":1,"unit":"$"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Calculate the value after 6 years, to the nearest dollar.","kind":"numeric","points":3,"hints":["0.85^6 ≈ 0.37715.","24000 × 0.37715."],"strand":"D(iii)","answer":9052,"tolerance":3,"unit":"$"},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"State the number of complete years before the value first falls below $8 000.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Your 6-year value is still above 8000.","Multiply by 0.85 once more and compare."],"strand":"D(iv)","answer":7,"tolerance":0,"unit":"years"}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 3 marks: 1 for the power, 1 for 0.614125, 1 for $14 739. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the sixth power, 1 for the product, 1 for $9 052. (d) 2 marks: 1 for the 7th year value, 1 for 7.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 3 marks: 1 for the power, 1 for 0.614125, 1 for $14 739. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the sixth power, 1 for the product, 1 for $9 052. (d) 2 marks: 1 for the 7th year value, 1 for 7.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","table"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Tabulate the year-by-year values in the workspace; (d) needs the bracketing years, not a guess."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 3 marks: 1 for the power, 1 for 0.614125, 1 for $14 739. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the sixth power, 1 for the product, 1 for $9 052. (d) 2 marks: 1 for the 7th year value, 1 for 7."},{"n":33,"t":"assess_parts","p":"In a geometric sequence the first term is 8 and the common ratio is 1/2.\n\n(a) Write the first four terms.\n(b) Calculate the sum of the first 6 terms.\n(c) Calculate the sum to infinity.\n(d) Explain why the sum of the first 6 terms is already close to the sum to infinity.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In a geometric sequence the first term is 8 and the common ratio is 1/2."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Write the first four terms.\n(b) Calculate the sum of the first 6 terms.\n(c) Calculate the sum to infinity.\n(d) Explain why the sum of the first 6 terms is already close to the sum to infinity."}]}]},"e":"Terms 8, 4, 2, 1, 0.5, 0.25 give S_6 = 15.75 and S∞ = 16; the missing tail is exactly 0.25, the seventh term onwards, which halves away rapidly.","m":9,"df":"hard","tp":"geometric sequences","st":"geometric sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":33,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"For |r| < 1 the tail after n terms is r^n/(1 − r) times the first term: it shrinks geometrically.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"State the 4th term.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Multiply by 1/2 for each successive term.","8, 4, 2, then one more halving."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":1,"tolerance":0},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the sum of the first 6 terms.","kind":"numeric","points":3,"hints":["Use S_n = a(1 − r^n)/(1 − r).","8(1 − 1/64)/(0.5)."],"strand":"D(ii)","answer":15.75,"tolerance":0.02},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Calculate the sum to infinity.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["S∞ = a/(1 − r) because |r| < 1.","8 ÷ 0.5."],"strand":"D(iii)","answer":16,"tolerance":0},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Explain why six terms already give almost the whole sum.","kind":"text","points":3,"hints":["Compare your two sums.","What size is the remaining tail after six terms?"],"strand":"D(iv)","accepted":["the sixth term is only 0.25 and every later term is smaller still, so the tail beyond six terms adds just 0.25 to reach 16"],"keywords":[["sixth"],["term"],["later"],["smaller"],["tail"],["beyond"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 3 marks: 1 for the formula, 1 for 63/64, 1 for 15.75. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the formula, 1 for 16. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the comparison, 1 for the 0.25 tail, 1 for the halving argument.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 3 marks: 1 for the formula, 1 for 63/64, 1 for 15.75. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the formula, 1 for 16. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the comparison, 1 for the 0.25 tail, 1 for the halving argument.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Keep the fraction 63/64 in (b) until the last step; the exact form makes (d) easy."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 3 marks: 1 for the formula, 1 for 63/64, 1 for 15.75. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the formula, 1 for 16. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the comparison, 1 for the 0.25 tail, 1 for the halving argument."},{"n":34,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A ladder has rungs whose lengths form an arithmetic sequence. The bottom rung is 60 cm and each rung above it is 2 cm shorter. There are 14 rungs.\n\n(a) State the length of the top rung.\n(b) Calculate the total length of timber needed for all 14 rungs.\n(c) Explain why the average rung length is the mean of the top and bottom rungs.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A ladder has rungs whose lengths form an arithmetic sequence. The bottom rung is 60 cm and each rung above it is 2 cm shorter. There are 14 rungs."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) State the length of the top rung.\n(b) Calculate the total length of timber needed for all 14 rungs.\n(c) Explain why the average rung length is the mean of the top and bottom rungs."}]}]},"e":"Top rung 34 cm gives S_14 = 7(94) = 658 cm and a mean of 47 cm. In an arithmetic sequence the symmetric pairs all have the same total, so the mean is the average of the end terms.","m":8,"df":"hard","tp":"arithmetic sequences","st":"arithmetic sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":34,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","strand":"C(i)","next_time_hint":"An arithmetic mean of the end terms works because of the symmetry of equal steps; say so explicitly.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"State the length of the top rung, in centimetres.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["13 steps of 2 cm separate the 1st and 14th rungs.","60 − 2(13)."],"strand":"C(i)","answer":34,"tolerance":0,"unit":"cm"},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the total length of timber, in centimetres.","kind":"numeric","points":3,"hints":["The rung lengths form an arithmetic series: S_n = n/2 × (first + last).","There are 14 rungs, so evaluate 7 × 94."],"strand":"C(ii)","answer":658,"tolerance":0,"unit":"cm"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"State the mean rung length, in centimetres.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["The mean is the total divided by 14.","Or use (60 + 34)/2."],"strand":"C(iii)","answer":47,"tolerance":0,"unit":"cm"},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Explain why the mean equals the average of the end rungs.","kind":"text","points":3,"hints":["Write the terms and pair the first with the last.","Do the pairs have equal sums?"],"strand":"C(iv)","accepted":["in an arithmetic sequence the terms are evenly spaced, so first plus last equals second plus second-last and so on; every pair has the same sum, making the mean the average of the two end terms"],"keywords":[["arithmetic"],["sequence"],["terms"],["evenly"],["spaced"],["first"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 3 marks: 1 for the formula, 1 for 94, 1 for 658. (c) 1 mark. (d) 3 marks: 1 for even spacing, 1 for the pairing, 1 for the conclusion.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 3 marks: 1 for the formula, 1 for 94, 1 for 658. (c) 1 mark. (d) 3 marks: 1 for even spacing, 1 for the pairing, 1 for the conclusion.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"The pairing argument in (d) is what earns the marks — not a numerical example."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 3 marks: 1 for the formula, 1 for 94, 1 for 658. (c) 1 mark. (d) 3 marks: 1 for even spacing, 1 for the pairing, 1 for the conclusion."},{"n":35,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A town''s population P after t years is modelled as P = 12 000 × 1.03^t.\n\n(a) State the population growth rate per year.\n(b) Calculate the population after 10 years, to the nearest whole person.\n(c) Calculate the time for the population to double, to 2 decimal places.\n(d) State one limitation of this model.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A town''s population P after t years is modelled as P = 12 000 × 1.03^t."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) State the population growth rate per year.\n(b) Calculate the population after 10 years, to the nearest whole person.\n(c) Calculate the time for the population to double, to 2 decimal places.\n(d) State one limitation of this model."}]}]},"e":"The multiplier 1.03 is a 3% annual increase. P(10) ≈ 16 127. Doubling: t = log 2 / log 1.03 ≈ 23.45 years. The model assumes unlimited growth at a constant rate, which no real town sustains.","m":9,"df":"hard","tp":"modelling","st":"modelling","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":35,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"For an exponential model, the base gives the rate and logarithms give the time to reach a target.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"State the annual growth rate, as a percentage.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Subtract 1 from the multiplier 1.03.","0.03 as a percentage."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":3,"tolerance":0,"unit":"%"},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the population after 10 years.","kind":"numeric","points":3,"hints":["Substitute t = 10.","1.03^10 ≈ 1.3439."],"strand":"D(ii)","answer":16127,"tolerance":2,"unit":"people"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Calculate the doubling time, in years, to 2 decimal places.","kind":"numeric","points":3,"hints":["Solve 1.03^t = 2 using logarithms.","t = log 2 ÷ log 1.03."],"strand":"D(iii)","answer":23.45,"tolerance":0.05,"unit":"years"},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"State and justify one limitation of the model.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Does a real population grow at a constant rate forever?","What about migration, housing or resources?"],"strand":"D(iv)","accepted":["it assumes a constant 3% growth forever, but real populations are limited by housing, resources and migration, so the rate will not stay constant"],"keywords":[["assumes"],["constant"],["growth"],["forever"],["real"],["populations"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 3 marks: 1 for substitution, 1 for 1.03^10, 1 for 16 127. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the log equation, 1 for the evaluated logs, 1 for 23.45. (d) 2 marks: 1 for a valid limitation, 1 for justification.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 3 marks: 1 for substitution, 1 for 1.03^10, 1 for 16 127. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the log equation, 1 for the evaluated logs, 1 for 23.45. (d) 2 marks: 1 for a valid limitation, 1 for justification.","response_tools":["graph","rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"graph","response_guidance":"Plot the model in the workspace to check the doubling time visually before computing it."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 3 marks: 1 for substitution, 1 for 1.03^10, 1 for 16 127. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the log equation, 1 for the evaluated logs, 1 for 23.45. (d) 2 marks: 1 for a valid limitation, 1 for justification."},{"n":36,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A rectangle has a perimeter of 40 cm. Its length is x cm.\n\n(a) Write the width in terms of x.\n(b) Show that the area is A = 20x − x².\n(c) State the value of x that maximises the area.\n(d) Calculate the maximum area.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A rectangle has a perimeter of 40 cm. Its length is x cm."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Write the width in terms of x.\n(b) Show that the area is A = 20x − x².\n(c) State the value of x that maximises the area.\n(d) Calculate the maximum area."}]}]},"e":"Width 20 − x gives A = 20x − x², and completing the square gives 100 − (x − 10)², so the maximum area is 100 cm² at x = 10: a square.","m":7,"df":"hard","tp":"quadratic functions","st":"quadratic functions","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":36,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"A downward parabola y = −(x − h)² + k peaks at x = h; completing the square locates it exactly.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"State the width when the length is x, in centimetres.","kind":"expression","points":1,"hints":["The two lengths and two widths total 40.","So one length plus one width is 20."],"strand":"D(i)","accepted":["20 - x"]},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"State the coefficient of x in the area expression 20x − x².","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["The area is length × width.","Multiply out x(20 − x)."],"strand":"D(ii)","answer":20,"tolerance":0},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"State the length that maximises the area, in centimetres.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Complete the square: A = 100 − (x − 10)².","The squared term is smallest when it is zero."],"strand":"D(iii)","answer":10,"tolerance":0,"unit":"cm"},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Calculate the maximum area, in square centimetres.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Substitute x = 10 into 20x − x².","200 − 100."],"strand":"D(iv)","answer":100,"tolerance":0,"unit":"cm²"},{"id":"e","label":"(e)","prompt":"Describe the shape of the rectangle with the maximum area.","kind":"text","points":1,"hints":["What are its length and width at the maximum?","A rectangle with equal sides has a special name."],"strand":"D(iv)","accepted":["a square with sides of 10 cm"]}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 1 mark. (c) 2 marks: 1 for completing the square, 1 for x = 10. (d) 2 marks: 1 for substituting, 1 for 100. (e) 1 mark.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 1 mark. (c) 2 marks: 1 for completing the square, 1 for x = 10. (d) 2 marks: 1 for substituting, 1 for 100. (e) 1 mark.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","graph"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Complete the square in (c); it proves the maximum rather than assuming the midpoint."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 1 mark. (c) 2 marks: 1 for completing the square, 1 for x = 10. (d) 2 marks: 1 for substituting, 1 for 100. (e) 1 mark."},{"n":37,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A loan of $6 000 charges 1.5% interest per month on the outstanding balance. No repayments are made for 8 months.\n\n(a) State the monthly multiplier.\n(b) Calculate the amount owed after 8 months, to the nearest cent.\n(c) Calculate the total interest charged. Explain why this exceeds 8 × 1.5% of $6 000.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A loan of "},{"type":"text","text":"$6 000 charges 1.5% interest per month on the outstanding balance. No repayments are made for 8 months."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) State the monthly multiplier.\n(b) Calculate the amount owed after 8 months, to the nearest cent.\n(c) Calculate the total interest charged. Explain why this exceeds 8 × 1.5% of "},{"type":"text","text":"$6 000."}]}]},"e":"6000 × 1.015^8 = $6 758.94, so the interest is $758.94 against $720 for constant simple charges, because the balance compounds monthly.","m":8,"df":"hard","tp":"compound interest","st":"compound interest","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":37,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"Monthly compounding multiplies the balance repeatedly, so the total interest exceeds the sum of the simple monthly rates.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"State the monthly multiplier.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["A 1.5% charge adds 1.5% of the balance.","Write 101.5% as a decimal."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":1.015,"tolerance":0.0005},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the amount owed after 8 months, to the nearest cent.","kind":"numeric","points":3,"hints":["Use 6000 × 1.015^8.","1.015^8 ≈ 1.12649."],"strand":"D(ii)","answer":6758.94,"tolerance":0.05,"unit":"$"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Calculate the total interest charged.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Interest paid is the amount owed minus the money borrowed.","Subtract $6 000 from your 8-month balance."],"strand":"D(iii)","answer":758.94,"tolerance":0.05,"unit":"$"},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Explain why the interest exceeds 8 × 1.5% of $6 000.","kind":"text","points":3,"hints":["What balance is each month''s 1.5% charged on?","Does the balance stay at $6 000?"],"strand":"D(iv)","accepted":["each month''s interest is charged on a balance that already includes earlier interest, so the interest itself earns interest and the total exceeds the $720 a constant charge would give"],"keywords":[["month''s"],["interest"],["charged"],["balance"],["already"],["includes"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 3 marks: 1 for the power, 1 for 1.12649, 1 for $6 758.94. (c) 1 mark. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the growing balance, 1 for the $720 comparison, 1 for the consequence.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 3 marks: 1 for the power, 1 for 1.12649, 1 for $6 758.94. (c) 1 mark. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the growing balance, 1 for the $720 comparison, 1 for the consequence.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Give the exact formula in (b) and compare with $720 explicitly in (d)."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 3 marks: 1 for the power, 1 for 1.12649, 1 for $6 758.94. (c) 1 mark. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the growing balance, 1 for the $720 comparison, 1 for the consequence."},{"n":38,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A fractal design is built from squares. The first square has area 64 cm². Each new square has 1/4 of the area of the previous one, and the design contains infinitely many squares.\n\n(a) State the area of the second square.\n(b) Calculate the total area of the first 3 squares.\n(c) Calculate the total area of the infinite design.\n(d) Explain why infinitely many squares occupy a finite area.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A fractal design is built from squares. The first square has area 64 cm². Each new square has 1/4 of the area of the previous one, and the design contains infinitely many squares."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) State the area of the second square.\n(b) Calculate the total area of the first 3 squares.\n(c) Calculate the total area of the infinite design.\n(d) Explain why infinitely many squares occupy a finite area."}]}]},"e":"Areas 64, 16, 4, … sum to 64/(1 − 0.25) = 85.33 cm². Because each increment is a quarter of the last, the total converges: an infinite number of squares with a finite combined area.","m":9,"df":"hard","tp":"limits of series","st":"limits of series","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":38,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","strand":"C(i)","next_time_hint":"An infinite geometric series with |r| < 1 converges; the sum is a/(1 − r).","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"State the area of the second square, in square centimetres.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Each square has a quarter of the area of the one before.","Divide 64 by 4."],"strand":"C(i)","answer":16,"tolerance":0,"unit":"cm²"},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the total area of the first three squares.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["The third square has area 4.","64 + 16 + 4."],"strand":"C(ii)","answer":84,"tolerance":0,"unit":"cm²"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Calculate the total area of the infinite design, to 2 decimal places.","kind":"numeric","points":3,"hints":["Use S∞ = a/(1 − r) with a = 64 and r = 0.25.","64 ÷ 0.75."],"strand":"C(iii)","answer":85.33,"tolerance":0.02,"unit":"cm²"},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Explain why infinitely many squares can occupy a finite area.","kind":"text","points":3,"hints":["How much does each new square add compared with the last?","Does the running total keep growing without limit?"],"strand":"C(iv)","accepted":["each new square adds only a quarter of the previous one, so the increments shrink geometrically and the running total converges to 85.33 cm² rather than growing without bound"],"keywords":[["square"],["adds"],["quarter"],["previous"],["increments"],["shrink"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the third term, 1 for 84. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the formula, 1 for 0.75, 1 for 85.33. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the shrinking increment, 1 for convergence, 1 for the finite limit.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the third term, 1 for 84. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the formula, 1 for 0.75, 1 for 85.33. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the shrinking increment, 1 for convergence, 1 for the finite limit.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Quote your running totals in (d) — the convergence is shown by the numbers, not by the word ''infinite''."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the third term, 1 for 84. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the formula, 1 for 0.75, 1 for 85.33. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the shrinking increment, 1 for convergence, 1 for the finite limit."},{"n":39,"t":"assess_parts","p":"Simplify and evaluate each expression, showing the index law used.\n\n(a) 3^4 × 3^2 ÷ 3^3\n(b) (5^3)^2 ÷ 5^4\n(c) 2^−3 × 2^5","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Simplify and evaluate each expression, showing the index law used."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) 3^4 × 3^2 ÷ 3^3\n(b) (5^3)^2 ÷ 5^4\n(c) 2^−3 × 2^5"}]}]},"e":"(a) 3^(4+2−3) = 3^3 = 27. (b) 5^(6−4) = 5^2 = 25. (c) 2^(−3+5) = 2^2 = 4. The laws are: add indices when multiplying, subtract when dividing, multiply when raising a power to a power.","m":7,"df":"hard","tp":"indices","st":"indices","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":39,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","strand":"C(i)","next_time_hint":"Handle the indices first, then evaluate the single remaining power.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Evaluate 3^4 × 3^2 ÷ 3^3.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Multiply by adding the indices, divide by subtracting.","4 + 2 − 3 = 3, so the answer is 3^3."],"strand":"C(i)","answer":27,"tolerance":0},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Evaluate (5^3)^2 ÷ 5^4.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["A power of a power multiplies the indices: (5^3)^2 = 5^6.","Then subtract the index 4."],"strand":"C(ii)","answer":25,"tolerance":0},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Evaluate 2^−3 × 2^5.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Add the indices: −3 + 5 = 2.","Then evaluate 2^2."],"strand":"C(iii)","answer":4,"tolerance":0},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"State the index law used to simplify 2^−3 × 2^5, in the form a^m × a^n = …","kind":"expression","points":1,"hints":["What happens to the indices when powers of the same base are multiplied?","State the law in the form a^m × a^n = …"],"strand":"C(iii)","accepted":["add the indices when multiplying powers of the same base"],"keywords":[["indices"],["multiplying"],["powers"],["base"]],"minKeywordGroups":3}],"marking":"(a) 2 marks: 1 for the combined index, 1 for 27. (b) 2 marks: 1 for 5^6, 1 for 25. (c) 2 marks: 1 for 2^2, 1 for 4. (d) 1 mark.","marking_criteria":"(a) 2 marks: 1 for the combined index, 1 for 27. (b) 2 marks: 1 for 5^6, 1 for 25. (c) 2 marks: 1 for 2^2, 1 for 4. (d) 1 mark.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Write each law before applying it; the final values alone will not earn full marks."},"marking":"(a) 2 marks: 1 for the combined index, 1 for 27. (b) 2 marks: 1 for 5^6, 1 for 25. (c) 2 marks: 1 for 2^2, 1 for 4. (d) 1 mark."},{"n":40,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A runner''s weekly distance increases by 10% each week. She runs 12 km in week 1.\n\n(a) Calculate the distance in week 4, to 2 decimal places.\n(b) Calculate the total distance run in the first 6 weeks.\n(c) Explain why this plan cannot continue indefinitely, using a calculation for week 30.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A runner''s weekly distance increases by 10% each week. She runs 12 km in week 1."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate the distance in week 4, to 2 decimal places.\n(b) Calculate the total distance run in the first 6 weeks.\n(c) Explain why this plan cannot continue indefinitely, using a calculation for week 30."}]}]},"e":"Week 4 is 12 × 1.1³ = 15.97 km; the six-week total is 92.59 km. Week 30 would be 12 × 1.1^29 ≈ 190 km, which is not physically sustainable, so the model breaks down.","m":10,"df":"hard","tp":"modelling","st":"modelling","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":40,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"An exponential model may fit the early data yet fail eventually; always test it at a distant value.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Calculate the distance in week 4, to 2 decimal places.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["The multiplier is 1.1 and week 1 is the starting point.","12 × 1.1^3."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":15.97,"tolerance":0.02,"unit":"km"},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the total distance for the first 6 weeks, to 2 decimal places.","kind":"numeric","points":3,"hints":["Use S_n = a(r^n − 1)/(r − 1) with r = 1.1.","12(1.1^6 − 1)/0.1."],"strand":"D(ii)","answer":92.59,"tolerance":0.05,"unit":"km"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Calculate the weekly distance predicted for week 30, to the nearest kilometre.","kind":"numeric","points":3,"hints":["Use 12 × 1.1^29.","1.1^29 ≈ 15.86."],"strand":"D(iii)","answer":190,"tolerance":2,"unit":"km"},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Explain why the plan cannot continue indefinitely.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Compare 190 km per week with what a person can run.","Can growth at 10% per week be sustained?"],"strand":"D(iv)","accepted":["week 30 would demand about 190 km in a week, far beyond sustainable human training, so the exponential model must fail: real training plateaus"],"keywords":[["week"],["demand"],["beyond"],["sustainable"],["human"],["training"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 2 marks: 1 for 1.1^3, 1 for 15.97. (b) 3 marks: 1 for the sum formula, 1 for 0.771561, 1 for 92.59. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the power, 1 for 15.86, 1 for 190. (d) 2 marks: 1 for the impossible distance, 1 for the conclusion.","marking_criteria":"(a) 2 marks: 1 for 1.1^3, 1 for 15.97. (b) 3 marks: 1 for the sum formula, 1 for 0.771561, 1 for 92.59. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the power, 1 for 15.86, 1 for 190. (d) 2 marks: 1 for the impossible distance, 1 for the conclusion.","response_tools":["graph","rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"graph","response_guidance":"Plot the weekly distances in the workspace; the shape is the argument for (d)."},"marking":"(a) 2 marks: 1 for 1.1^3, 1 for 15.97. (b) 3 marks: 1 for the sum formula, 1 for 0.771561, 1 for 92.59. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the power, 1 for 15.86, 1 for 190. (d) 2 marks: 1 for the impossible distance, 1 for the conclusion."},{"n":41,"t":"assess_parts","p":"An investment of $3 000 grows at 6% per year compound interest.\n\n(a) Write the value after n years as an expression.\n(b) Calculate the value after 12 years, to the nearest cent.\n(c) Using logarithms, calculate the number of years for the investment to reach $6 000, to 2 decimal places.\n(d) Explain why the doubling time is the same whatever the initial amount.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"An investment of "},{"type":"text","text":"$3 000 grows at 6% per year compound interest."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Write the value after n years as an expression.\n(b) Calculate the value after 12 years, to the nearest cent.\n(c) Using logarithms, calculate the number of years for the investment to reach "},{"type":"text","text":"$6 000, to 2 decimal places.\n(d) Explain why the doubling time is the same whatever the initial amount."}]}]},"e":"3000(1.06)^12 = $6 036.59. Doubling solves 1.06^n = 2, giving n ≈ 11.90 years; the factor 2 is independent of the amount invested, so the doubling time depends only on the rate.","m":10,"df":"hard","tp":"exponential equations","st":"exponential equations","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":41,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"Growth factors are ratios, so doubling times depend on the rate alone — divide out the initial amount.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Calculate the value after 12 years, to the nearest cent.","kind":"numeric","points":3,"hints":["Use 3000 × 1.06^12.","1.06^12 ≈ 2.0122."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":6036.59,"tolerance":0.05,"unit":"$"},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"State the value of 6000 ÷ 3000, the factor by which the money must grow.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Divide the target by the starting amount.","This equals 1.06^n."],"strand":"D(ii)","answer":2,"tolerance":0},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Calculate the number of years to double, to 2 decimal places.","kind":"numeric","points":3,"hints":["Solve 1.06^n = 2 with logarithms.","n = log 2 ÷ log 1.06."],"strand":"D(iii)","answer":11.9,"tolerance":0.05,"unit":"years"},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Explain why the doubling time does not depend on the initial amount.","kind":"text","points":3,"hints":["Write the doubling equation with P as the starting amount.","Does P cancel?"],"strand":"D(iv)","accepted":["doubling requires P(1.06)^n = 2P, and dividing both sides by P leaves 1.06^n = 2, so the starting amount cancels and only the rate matters"],"keywords":[["doubling"],["requires"],["p106n"],["dividing"],["sides"],["leaves"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 3 marks: 1 for the power, 1 for 2.0122, 1 for $6 036.59. (b) 1 mark. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the log equation, 1 for the evaluated logs, 1 for 11.90. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the equation with P, 1 for the cancellation, 1 for the conclusion.","marking_criteria":"(a) 3 marks: 1 for the power, 1 for 2.0122, 1 for $6 036.59. (b) 1 mark. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the log equation, 1 for the evaluated logs, 1 for 11.90. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the equation with P, 1 for the cancellation, 1 for the conclusion.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Write the doubling equation with a symbol P in (d); the cancellation is the whole argument."},"marking":"(a) 3 marks: 1 for the power, 1 for 2.0122, 1 for $6 036.59. (b) 1 mark. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the log equation, 1 for the evaluated logs, 1 for 11.90. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the equation with P, 1 for the cancellation, 1 for the conclusion."}]'::jsonb) as x
